<div class="help-article">
    <p class="help-lead">
        When you're ready to file, or to hand things to your accountant, the <a href="tax">Tax report</a> gives you a printable
        summary and a CSV file of the year's claims.
    </p>

    <h3>Printable summary</h3>
    <p>
        <span class="help-ui">Printable summary</span> opens a clean page of the year's <strong>confirmed</strong> claims: a table of totals by CRA
        line, then an appendix listing every transaction behind them with its date, account, description, note, receipt file
        name and amount (or the business-use share counted). <span class="help-ui">Print or save as PDF</span> opens your browser's
        print window; choose <em>Save as PDF</em> as the printer for a file.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Suggestions not yet reviewed are left out, and the summary says how many there are.</li>
        <li>Confirmed claims without a receipt are marked <em>missing</em>.</li>
        <li>With a household, pick the person on the Tax report first to print just their claims.</li>
        <li>RRSP contributions from the first 60 days of the next year are noted but not counted, unless you moved them to this year.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Export CSV</h3>
    <p>
        <span class="help-ui">Export CSV</span> downloads a spreadsheet file (it opens in Excel, Numbers or Google Sheets) with one row per
        transaction on the report: tax category, CRA line, status (confirmed or still suggested), date, account, description,
        amount, note, receipts, who claims it, business-use percent and the amount claimed. Filter on the status column to keep
        only confirmed claims. Like the summary, it follows the person picked on the report.
    </p>
    <p class="help-note">
        The amounts are what you paid (or the share you set), not the deduction or credit you'll receive. Enter them in your tax
        software or give them to your preparer along with the official slips and receipts.
    </p>
</div>
